[Music]  it's safe to say that we use had a  sparse year with the switch on the way  Nintendo's clearly shifting its  development focus elsewhere since the  term of the year I so love around 150  games of release for the Wii U but it's  difficult to name more than a handful as  indie darlings like odd world Abe's  Oddysee new and tasty old reliable the  Lego titles like Lego Star Wars The  Force awakens and the odd big  third-party port like Minecraft story  mode but nothing really huge immediately  comes to mind luckily for you  game SWAT has done the digging for you  so let's take a look at the 5 best games  the Wii U had to offer and its last full  year in the limelight pokken tournament  is a bizarre pokemon Tekken crossover  and offers Pokemon found something  they've been dreaming of since the  series inception in 1996 characters  actually fighting until now Pokemon  games had only shown the monsters  performing small movements in battle  pokken tournament finally showed series  favorites like chars island Jenga  physically heating each other giving the  fighting game a sense of impact that  some say the franchise had lacked it  stayed faithful to the game's tactical  roots too with mega evolutions support  characters and a two-phase attack system  meaning you'll need to be quick in your  head  as well as in your fingers to succeed  and it does all this without  overwhelming your players which is  important because although it's  definitely a hardcore fighting game many  people playing will simply be big fans  of Pikachu just like you and me  next up is another crossover this time  between Shin Megami Tensei and Fire  Emblem Tokyo Mirage sessions takes the  plot and relationship building of a  Megami Tensei or persona game but the  rest is unmistakably Fire Emblem so  while demons are attempting to take over  the world and you've got to make friends  to beat them the characters weapons and  skill upgrades are all from Fire Emblem  a crossover like that could have felt  weird with art styles and gameplay  systems clashing but developer Atlas has  beautifully merged two games into a  great dungeon crawler in fact the  characters and mechanics are married so  well and this strange mashups tsoi's it  feels like the two franchises were  always destined to be together you might  not have heard of axiom verge but it's  easily one of the best Wii U games this  year  it's a side-scrolling indie Metroidvania  developed by just one man all by himself  being made by one man army didn't hinder  axiom though it's mysterious plot soon  drools you in before knew perfectly  balanced mechanics and items push you on  small drones grappling hooks and laser  drills allow you to explore new parts of  the world if you're good enough that is  the amount of Secrets you discover even  affects the ending you get so there's a  reason to go back again and again it's  well worth a playthrough or five another  indie game reaches the top 5 in steam  world heist if you're unaware  imagine XCOM as a 2d side-scroller and  your crew is a ragtag squad of robots  each character has a unique special  ability plus their own kooky sense of  humor and if we mention the robots are  powered by steam yeah they're pretty  cool but it's the fact that it's XCOM in  2d that's the real hook here  not many strategy games are presented  this way but somehow it works  weapons lack any auto aim - so an  element of skill comes in to the  shooting so you can't blame it on a dice  roll if you miss  last up we've got Paper Mario Color  Splash a game that seemed to fly under a  lot of people's radars when it launched  in October some fans were upset that it  appeared similar in style to Paper Mario  sticker star on 3ds happily however  color splashes are delight it cuts down  on the text-heavy nature of some  previous Mario RPGs and even has too  deep a perfects to rival tear away above  all it brings a sense of humor to many  games forget and much like Nintendo  itself is heartwarming to the core  [Music]

